<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pavel Kiselyov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pavel Dmitrievich Kiselyov, Kiseleff</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Count Pavel Kiselyov was a Russian officer and statesman, generally regarded as the most brilliant reformer of the conservative reign of Nicholas I. After serving at Borodino and marching to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, he governed Wallachia and Moldavia as plenipotentiary president of their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divan">Divans</a> from 1829 to 1834, when the two got their first constitutions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic">Organic Statute</a>, in 1831 and 1832. As Minister of State Properties for 18 years he reformed the administration of state-owned peasants and set up schools for their children, while his plans to emancipate the serfs were laid to rest. Sent to Paris as envoy after the Crimean War, he retired in 1862 and died there in 1872.</p></div>
